C similar (~0.97-0.998), but d1vd2 and d2vd3 AUC much better for RF.\n- **Section 4.3**: Feature universality: p90_ppl and var_surprisal are universal top-4 features across all 9 cells.\n- **Section 4.4**: 3-class accuracy (excluding d0): 59-82%, confirming ordinal signal beyond human/machine boundary.\n- **Section 6**: Cross-strategy challenge: naive transfer 59.1% mean. Feature trajectory reversal for temperature. Two-stage pipeline (strategy ID → conditioned depth classifier) improves to 70.5% (+11.5pp). Strategy classifier accuracy: 57.8% (chance=33%). Oracle pipeline: 78.3%.\n- **Table 4 (Section 7.1)**: Qwen2.5-14B, nucleus only: 68% accuracy, d1vd2 AUC 0.76, d2vd3 AUC 0.67. Δ vs 1B best: -15.9pp. Feature overlap 2/4.\n- **Table 5 (Section 7.2)**: API/instruct models: GPT-4o-mini fine-tuned 45%, prompted 42%. Signal attenuated but non-zero.\n- **Table 6 (Section 7.3)**: Wild data FPR: C4 32%, RedPajama 25%, FineWeb 19%, WikiText control 1%. False positives concentrate in d0→d1 misclassification.\n- **Section 8**: Theoretical Motivation: tail erosion connection to Shumailov 2024, EDD upper bound (≤6 for 1B, empirical 3-4), strategy convergence rates. Explicitly labeled \"motivation not contribution.\"\n- **Section 9**: DCOL negative result: RoBERTa-large contrastive ordinal learning failed (64.4% vs RF 78.2%).\n- **Section 10.1 Limitations**: Honest about scale, cross-strategy, base model assumption, FPR, controlled setting.\n\n## Review Context (imagined experiments from review_context.yaml)\n\nThe planned experiments include:\n1. 14B_scale_validation (plan_014): Qwen2.5-14B, nucleus only, d0-d3. ✓ Covered in Section 7.1.\n2. wild_data_calibration (plan_015): C4/FineWeb/RedPajama/WikiText control. ✓ Covered in Section 7.3.\n3. detector_ordinal_baseline (plan_018): Binoculars + Fast-DetectGPT ordinal calibration. ✓ Covered in Section 5.\n4. bootstrap_confidence_intervals (plan_020): 95% CIs for Table 1. ✓ Covered.\n5. theoretical_analysis_section (plan_023): Theory section. ✓ Covered in Section 8.\n6. api_model_recursive_chains (plan_026): GPT-4o-mini API chains. ✓ Covered in Section 7.2.\n7. dcol_component_ablation (plan_010): Cancelled (DCOL failed). ✓ Negative result in Section 9.\n\nAll planned experiments appear covered.\n\n## Your Task\n\nProduce a structured review report:\n1. **Summary** (3-4 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bullet points, focus on methods/experiments)\n3. **Weaknesses** (each labeled Major/Minor + idea/presentation layer)\n   - Major issue definition: experiment scope insufficient for claims, core experiment unreproducible, statistically insignificant results claimed as contributions\n   - For each Major, explain WHY it's Major not Minor\n4. **Questions for Authors** (2-4 questions)\n5. **Score** (1-6 scale, 1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept; calibrate against top ML venue ~25% acceptance)\n6. **Confidence** (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. The paper's honest limitations should be acknowledged as a strength, but shouldn't immunize weak claims from critique.","uuid":"c9a3b1d9-bf3d-441e-8aa8-63ffe7a7d833","session_id":"54767580-3114-45ce-b1df-d1aec2ac2b43"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 1 in a paper review roundtable. Your focus is **methods + experiments**. Do NOT evaluate novelty or related work (that's Reviewer 2's job). Do NOT evaluate formatting.\n\n## Paper Summary (I've read the full 12-page PDF)\n\nTitle: \"Ordinal Depth Estimation for Recursive Model-Generated Text: A Depth Degradation Atlas\"\n\nThe paper formalizes ordinal generation depth estimation as a new task: given a text passage and a reference model, predict its recursive generation depth d ∈ {0,1,2,...,K} where d=0 is human-written text. They construct a benchmark with 3 model families (Pythia-1.4B, OLMo-1B, GPT-2 XL) × 3 decoding strategies (nucleus p=0.95, temperature τ=0.9, top-k k=50) × 4 depths (d0-d3), totaling 36 conditions. Each depth corpus = 5,000 passages of 256 tokens, generated via recursive LoRA fine-tuning (rank 16) on WikiText-103.\n\n19 statistical features extracted from each passage using a fixed reference model (GPT-2 Large 774M):\n- Perplexity statistics (7): mean, median, p90, std, IQR, passage-level ppl, cross-entropy\n- Surprisal statistics (6): mean, variance, skewness, kurtosis, range, std\n- Diversity statistics (6): type-token ratio, 3-gram/4-gram repetition, distinct-2 ratio, mean token prob, prob std\n\n4 classifiers evaluated: RF (500 trees), LR with L2, CORAL ordinal regression, DivEye adapted.\n\nKey Results:\n- **Table 1**: RF 4-class accuracy: 69.6% (Pythia/top-k) to 85.4% (GPT-2 XL/temp), all with 95% bootstrap CIs. Strategy mean: temp 81.4% > nucleus 80.3% > top-k 73.2%.\n- **Table 2**: EDD at T=0.75 is ≥3 in 7/9 settings, meaning d0-d3 are pairwise distinguishable in most settings.\n- **Table 3**: Comparison with binary detectors calibrated for ordinal task: RF 78.3% mean vs Fast-DetectGPT 54.0% vs Binoculars 48.0%. d0vd1 AUC similar (~0.97-0.998), but d1vd2 and d2vd3 AUC much better for RF.\n- **Section 4.3**: Feature universality: p90_ppl and var_surprisal are universal top-4 features across all 9 cells.\n- **Section 4.4**: 3-class accuracy (excluding d0): 59-82%, confirming ordinal signal beyond human/machine boundary.\n- **Section 6**: Cross-strategy challenge: naive transfer 59.1% mean. Feature trajectory reversal for temperature. Two-stage pipeline (strategy ID → conditioned depth classifier) improves to 70.5% (+11.5pp). Strategy classifier accuracy: 57.8% (chance=33%). Oracle pipeline: 78.3%.\n- **Table 4 (Section 7.1)**: Qwen2.5-14B, nucleus only: 68% accuracy, d1vd2 AUC 0.76, d2vd3 AUC 0.67. Δ vs 1B best: -15.9pp. Feature overlap 2/4.\n- **Table 5 (Section 7.2)**: API/instruct models: GPT-4o-mini fine-tuned 45%, prompted 42%. Signal attenuated but non-zero.\n- **Table 6 (Section 7.3)**: Wild data FPR: C4 32%, RedPajama 25%, FineWeb 19%, WikiText control 1%. False positives concentrate in d0→d1 misclassification.\n- **Section 8**: Theoretical Motivation: tail erosion connection to Shumailov 2024, EDD upper bound (≤6 for 1B, empirical 3-4), strategy convergence rates. Explicitly labeled \"motivation not contribution.\"\n- **Section 9**: DCOL negative result: RoBERTa-large contrastive ordinal learning failed (64.4% vs RF 78.2%).\n- **Section 10.1 Limitations**: Honest about scale, cross-strategy, base model assumption, FPR, controlled setting.\n\n## Review Context (imagined experiments from review_context.yaml)\n\nThe planned experiments include:\n1. 14B_scale_validation (plan_014): Qwen2.5-14B, nucleus only, d0-d3. ✓ Covered in Section 7.1.\n2. wild_data_calibration (plan_015): C4/FineWeb/RedPajama/WikiText control. ✓ Covered in Section 7.3.\n3. detector_ordinal_baseline (plan_018): Binoculars + Fast-DetectGPT ordinal calibration. ✓ Covered in Section 5.\n4. bootstrap_confidence_intervals (plan_020): 95% CIs for Table 1. ✓ Covered.\n5. theoretical_analysis_section (plan_023): Theory section. ✓ Covered in Section 8.\n6. api_model_recursive_chains (plan_026): GPT-4o-mini API chains. ✓ Covered in Section 7.2.\n7. dcol_component_ablation (plan_010): Cancelled (DCOL failed). ✓ Negative result in Section 9.\n\nAll planned experiments appear covered.\n\n## Your Task\n\nProduce a structured review report:\n1. **Summary** (3-4 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bullet points, focus on methods/experiments)\n3. **Weaknesses** (each labeled Major/Minor + idea/presentation layer)\n   - Major issue definition: experiment scope insufficient for claims, core experiment unreproducible, statistically insignificant results claimed as contributions\n   - For each Major, explain WHY it's Major not Minor\n4. **Questions for Authors** (2-4 questions)\n5. **Score** (1-6 scale, 1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept; calibrate against top ML venue ~25% acceptance)\n6. **Confidence** (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. The paper's honest limitations should be acknowledged as a strength, but shouldn't immunize weak claims from critique."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01PS8qzVjDvdR2bEc7jbYPyR","session_id":"54767580-3114-45ce-b1df-d1aec2ac2b43","uuid":"c13fe322-d139-4ecb-8fec-d810b1405dc3","timestamp":"2026-05-18T17:04:01.199Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01UEWF97BVa3T3q6sz8KVqXA","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LUkzsjrCQuHv8SbXSW5Ykq","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 in a paper review roundtable. Your focus is **novelty + related work + story consistency**. Do NOT evaluate method implementation details or experiment design (that's Reviewer 1's job). Do NOT evaluate formatting.\n\n## Paper Summary (I've read the full 12-page PDF)\n\nTitle: \"Ordinal Depth Estimation for Recursive Model-Generated Text: A Depth Degradation Atlas\"\n\nThe paper formalizes ordinal generation depth estimation as a new task: given a text passage and a reference model, predict its recursive generation depth d ∈ {0,1,...,K}. d=0 = human text. Benchmark: 3 models (Pythia-1.4B, OLMo-1B, GPT-2 XL) × 3 strategies (nucleus, temperature, top-k) × 4 depths × 5000 passages each. 19 statistical features (perplexity, surprisal, diversity). Random Forest is best classifier (70-85% 4-class accuracy).\n\n**Contributions claimed (Introduction p.2):**\n1. Task formalization and benchmark (3 models × 3 strategies × 4 depths, 70-85% accuracy)\n2. Depth Degradation Atlas: first systematic empirical characterization of recursive text degradation — (a) perplexity tail statistics as universal markers, (b) non-uniform inter-depth distances, (c) EDD boundary, (d) strategy-dependent feature trajectory reversal\n3. Cross-strategy pipeline: two-stage approach, +11.5pp over naive transfer\n\n**Related Work (Section 2, ~1 page):**\n- Model Collapse and Recursive Training: Shumailov 2024, Alemohammad 2024, Dohmatob 2024, Gerstgrasser 2024, Drayson 2025. Positioning: \"shifts from 'how does text degrade' to 'can we estimate depth from text alone.'\"\n- Machine-Generated Text Detection: Binoculars (Hans 2024), Fast-DetectGPT (Bao 2024), LOKI (Liu 2024), DivEye (He 2024), Uchendu 2020. Positioning: \"all designed for binary classification, not ordinal depth.\"\n- Decoding Strategy Effects: Holtzman 2020, Garces Arias 2025. Only 2 citations.\n- Data Curation and Quality Filtering: DSIR (Xie 2023), QuRating (Wettig 2024). Positioning: \"complementary signal.\"\n\nTotal references: ~28.\n\n**Key experimental findings:**\n- Within-strategy RF: 70-85% 4-class accuracy\n- Cross-strategy: naive 59.1%, two-stage pipeline 70.5%, oracle 78.3%\n- 14B (Qwen2.5-14B, nucleus only): 68% accuracy, attenuated signal\n- API/instruct: 42-45% accuracy (feature shift from p90_ppl to surprisal_range)\n- Wild data: 19-32% FPR on web-crawl, <2% on WikiText\n- Binary detector baselines: Fast-DetectGPT 54%, Binoculars 48% when calibrated for ordinal task\n- DCOL negative result: contrastive ordinal learning failed (64.4%)\n- Theoretical motivation: EDD bound, tail erosion connection (labeled \"motivation not contribution\")\n\n**Abstract claims:** \"70-85% four-class accuracy (chance=25%)\", \"preliminary experiments on Qwen2.5-14B confirm depth signal persistence at mainstream model scale (68% accuracy, chance=25%), with attenuated inter-depth discrimination compared to 1B models\"\n\n**Conclusion claims:** \"feature-based classifiers achieve 70-85% four-class accuracy\", \"Preliminary 14B results confirm signal persistence at larger scale, while honest reporting of limitations (API model attenuation, cross-strategy reversal, wild data FPR) delineates the boundaries of current feasibility\"\n\n## Your Task\n\nProduce a structured review report:\n1. **Summary** (3-4 sentences)\n2. **Strengths** (bullet points, focus on novelty/related work/story)\n3. **Weaknesses** (each labeled Major/Minor + idea/presentation layer)\n   - Major issue definition: novelty claim unverifiable due to missing related work, scope-venue mismatch, claims contradicted by own experiments\n   - For each Major, explain WHY it's Major not Minor\n4. **Questions for Authors** (2-4 questions)\n5. **Score** (1-6 scale, 1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept; calibrate against top ML venue ~25% acceptance)\n6. **Confidence** (1-5)\n\nKey considerations for your analysis:\n- Is \"task formalization + systematic empirical study\" sufficient novelty for a top ML venue without algorithmic innovation?\n- Does the Depth Degradation Atlas constitute a novel contribution or is it descriptive statistics?\n- Does the gap between binary detectors (48-54%) and the proposed method (78.3%) justify a separate paper, or is it incremental?\n- Are the story claims in Abstract/Intro consistent with what the experiments actually show?\n- Is the Related Work landscape sufficiently covered? Any critical missing areas (data decontamination, membership inference, corpus fingerprinting)?\n- How does the narrow practical scope (base models only, known strategy required, high wild FPR) affect the paper's positioning?\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Acknowledge genuine novelty in task formalization while being honest about methodological limitations.","model":"sonnet"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2153,"cache_read_input_tokens":55282,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2153},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"54767580-3114-45ce-b1df-d1aec2ac2b43","uuid":"58669c9f-305e-46b4-82d5-040665b1dec9"}
